  "Goals": [
    "Comprehend that cosine and sine are defined as the $x$- and $y$-coordinates of a point $P$ on the unit circle at $\\theta$ radians.",
    "Understand the Pythagorean Identity as a connection between the Pythagorean Theorem and the coordinates of points on a unit circle.",
    "Use the Pythagorean Identity to determine if a point is on the unit circle."
  ],
  "ElementCount": 32,
  "SourceElements": [
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L05-ACTIVITY-001",
      "SourceSection": "5.1: Circle Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Here is a circle centered at \\((0,0)\\) with a radius of 1 unit.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L05-ACTIVITY-002",
      "SourceSection": "5.1: Circle Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What are the exact coordinates of \\(P\\) if \\(P\\) is rotated counterclockwise \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\) radians from the point \\((1,0)\\)? Explain or show your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L05-ACTIVITY-003",
      "SourceSection": "5.2: Cosine, Sine, and the Unit Circle",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What are the exact coordinates of point \\(Q\\) if it is rotated \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\) radians counterclockwise from the point \\((1,0)\\)? Explain or show your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L05-ACTIVITY-004",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: A New Identity",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Is the point \\(\\left(\\text-0.5,\\sin(\\frac{4\\pi}{3})\\right)\\) on the unit circle? Explain or show your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L05-ACTIVITY-005",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: A New Identity",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Is the point \\(\\left(\\text-0.5,\\sin(\\frac{5\\pi}{6})\\right)\\) on the unit circle? Explain or show your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L05-ACTIVITY-006",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: A New Identity",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Suppose that \\(\\sin(\\theta)=\\text-0.5\\) and that \\(\\theta\\) is in quadrant 4. What is the exact value of \\(\\cos(\\theta)\\)? Explain or show your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L05-ACTIVITY-007",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: A New Identity",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Show that if \\(\\theta\\) is an angle between 0 and \\(2\\pi\\) and neither \\(\\cos(\\theta)=0\\) nor \\(\\sin(\\theta)=0\\), then it is impossible for the sum of \\(\\cos(\\theta)\\) and \\(\\sin(\\theta)\\) to be equal to 1.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L05-ACTIVITY-008",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: A New Identity",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Let’s say we have a point \\(P\\) with coordinates \\((a,b)\\) on the unit circle, like the one shown here:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L05-ACTIVITY-009",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: A New Identity",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Using the Pythagorean Theorem, we know that \\(a^2+b^2=1\\). We also know this is true using the equation for a circle with radius 1 unit, \\(x^2+y^2=1^2\\), which is true for the point \\((a,b)\\) since it is on the circle.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L05-ACTIVITY-010",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: A New Identity",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Another way to write the coordinates of \\(P\\) is using the angle \\(\\theta\\), which gives the location of \\(P\\) on the unit circle relative to the point \\((1,0)\\). Thinking of \\(P\\) this way, its coordinates are…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L05-ACTIVITY-011",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: A New Identity",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What if \\(\\theta\\) were a different angle and \\(P\\) wasn’t in quadrant 1? It turns out that no matter the quadrant, the coordinates of any point on the unit circle given by an angle \\(\\theta\\) are \\((\\cos(\\theta),\\sin(\\theta))\\). In fact,…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L05-ACTIVITY-012",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: A New Identity",
      "SourceExcerpt": "This revised definition of cosine and sine means that \\(\\cos^2(\\theta) + \\sin^2(\\theta) = 1\\) is true for all values of \\(\\theta\\) defined on the unit circle and is known as the Pythagorean Identity.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L05-ACTIVITY-013",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: A New Identity",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The identity \\(\\sin^2(x) + \\cos^2(x) = 1\\) relating the sine and cosine of a number. It is called the Pythagorean identity because it follows from the Pythagorean theorem.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L05-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: A New Identity",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Pythagorean identity",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L05-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: A New Identity",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The length of an interval at which a periodic function repeats. A function \\(f\\) has a period, \\(p\\), if \\(f(x+p) = f(x)\\) for all inputs \\(x\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L05-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: A New Identity",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A function whose values repeat at regular intervals. If \\(f\\) is a periodic function then there is a number \\(p\\), called the period, so that \\(f(x + p) = f(x)\\) for all inputs \\(x\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L05-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: A New Identity",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The circle in the coordinate plane with radius 1 and center the origin.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
